  <h3>Golden Age (1934–1960): The Foundational Orchestra</h3>
  <p>
    The Golden Age accounts for 27 wins—nearly 30% of the entire dataset. Composers like <strong>Max Steiner</strong>, <strong>Erich Wolfgang Korngold</strong>, and <strong>Alfred Newman</strong> established the symphonic Hollywood sound: sweeping strings, leitmotifs, and full orchestral palettes. The era’s 20 unique winners across 27 ceremonies yield a repeat-winner rate of 26%, the lowest of any era. The field was still open, and the conventions of film scoring were being written in real time.
  </p>

  <h3>Silver Age (1961–1980): Jazz, Pop, and the Auteur Composer</h3>
  <p>
    The Silver Age introduced greater stylistic variety. <strong>John Barry</strong> brought jazz-inflected romance, <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> offered epic Middle-Eastern and Russian textures, and <strong>Nino Rota</strong> channeled operatic Italian melody into <em>The Godfather</em>. The era also saw the first electronic winner: <strong>Giorgio Moroder</strong> for <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978), a harbinger of the synthesizer revolution. With 17 unique composers across 20 wins, the Silver Age maintained moderate diversity while allowing repeat winners like Jarre and Barry to build their legacies.
  </p>

  <h3>Modern Era (1981–2000): The Blockbuster and the Musical Renaissance</h3>
  <p>
    The Modern Era is defined by two opposing poles: the mega-orchestral blockbuster and the animated musical. <strong>John Williams</strong> scored the former (<em>Jaws</em>, <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler’s List</em>), while <strong>Alan Menken</strong> owned the latter (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>). The era’s 16 unique composers across 20 wins make it the most concentrated era, with a 20% repeat-winner rate driven largely by these two titans. It was also the last era in which a single composer could dominate both the box office and the Oscar stage.
  </p>

  <h3>Contemporary Era (2001–2026): Global Voices and Hybrid Scores</h3>
  <p>
    The Contemporary Era is the most globally diverse and stylistically eclectic. Winners include <strong>Tan Dun</strong> (Chinese, 2000), <strong>A.R. Rahman</strong> (Indian, 2008), <strong>Ludovic Bource</strong> (French, 2011), and <strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir</strong> (Icelandic, 2019). Electronic and ambient textures—pioneered by <strong>Trent Reznor & Atticus Ross</strong> (<em>The Social Network</em>, 2010; <em>Soul</em>, 2020)—now sit comfortably alongside traditional orchestration. Despite this diversity, the era has produced 20 unique composers across 26 wins, with Ludwig Göransson’s four victories creating a new center of gravity.
  </p>
</section>

